    "lawCode": "Add Art 5 Title 5-A §§1114-a - 1114-s, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S989\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to establishing\nthe North Shore water authority\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide authorization and statutory framework for the establishment\nof a North Shore Water Authority to operate in the service area present-\nly operated by a private utility in the northwest portion of Nassau\nCounty, servicing the residents of the Village of Sea Cliff, and\nportions of the village of Old Brookville, Village of Roslyn Harbor, and\nresidents in the unincorporated hamlets of Glenwood Landing, Glen Head\nand the City of Glen Cove.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection One contains the Legislative Findings.\n\nSection Two amends the Public Service Law by adding a new Title 5-A,\nentitled the North Shore Water Authority. Title 5-A contains nineteen\nsections, which establish the statutory scheme for the Water Authority.\nThese sections are as follows:\n\n1114-a: Provides that the act may be cited as the \"Water Authority of\nNorth Shore Act\"\n\n1114-b:Contains pertinent definitions.\n\n1114-c: Provides the water district boundaries.\n\n1114-d: Establishes the public benefit corporate structure and provides\nthe appointment process for the board of directors, public hearing\nrequirements before the purchase or condemnation of property within the\nservice area, non-binding referendum requirements, and weighting of the\nvotes cast by the board of directors. Further, this section contains the\nduties of the Board and the Officers appointed thereto.\n\n1114-e: Establishes the powers of the authority.\n\n1114-f: Provides that public officers and employees may be transferred\nto the authority upon consent of the transferring employer.\n\n1114-g: Authorizes the authority to issue bonds.\n\n1114-h: Specifies the remedies of bondholders in the event that the\nauthority defaults on a bond and provides that actions relating to\ndefault on bonds shall be adjudicated in Nassau County.\n\n1114-i: Provides that the state and municipalities thereof are not\nliable on bonds issued by the authority.\n\n1114-j: Governs monies of the authority.\n\n1114-k: Provides that the bonds of the authority shall be considered\nsecurities for investment and deposit.\n1114-1: Limits alterations by the state impacting bonds.\n\n1114-m: Exempts the authority from taxes and special levies or assess-\nments and allows for the establishment of PILOTS upon consent.\n\n1114-n: Relates to limitations on actions against the authority.\n\n1114-o: Governs conflicts of interest of members of the board of direc-\ntors and officers and employees of the authority.\n\n1114-p: Provides that contracts of the authority shall be let pursuant\nto the Wicks Law.\n\n1114-q: Contains a separability clause.\n\n1114-r: Governs inconsistent provisions.\n\n1114-t-Governs acquisition by the authority via eminent domain.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe ratepayers in the villages of Sea Cliff, Old Brookville, Roslyn\nHarbor and the hamlets of Glenwood Landing and Glen Head are currently\nserviced by American Water, a private water utility.  Unfortunately, a\nvariety of factors has led to exorbitant water rates for some customers\nand disruptions in service. This legislation would authorize, at local\noption, the establishment of a North Shore Water Authority. Upon forma-\ntion, the Authority would be authorized to take over the assets and\nfunctions currently provided by the private utility.\n\nPublic water authorities have been successful in protecting, preserving\nand enhancing the quality and quantity of water on Long Island. The\nformation of a public water authority should be an option for the\nimpacted localities to consider when evaluating possible remedies to\naddress the issues that have arisen with the private water utility.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019/20: S6491-B/A9674-A (Passed Senate)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
