    "lawCode": "Add Art 5 Title 4-A §§1092-a - 1092-v, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S989A\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to the assessment of property owned by water-works\ncorporations; and to amend the public authorities law, in relation to\ncreating the Nassau county water authority\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide authorization and statutory framework for the establishment\nof a Nassau County Water Authority and to exclude certain water-works\ncorporations from the special franchise tax.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 creates the Long Island water utility reform act.\n\nSection 2 exempts certain water-works corporations from the special\nfranchise tax.\n\nSection 3 limits the provisions of this act to water-works corporations\noperating in a county of one million or more and that have a four-class\nproperty tax system.\n\nSection 4 establishes the Nassau County Water Authority.\n\nSection 5 and 6 requires that any tax relief realized by this act shall\nbe used to reduce current water rates.\n\nSection 7 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nA recent study showed that 124,000 New York American Water customers are\ndisproportionately burdened by exorbitant rates for water and that muni-\ncipalization of the system is both feasible and in the public's inter-\nest. Private water utilities are subjected to income and property taxes,\nwhich are passed through to customers accounting for 38% - 60% of the\nwater bill for NYAW customers. This includes approximately $25 million\nper year in Special Franchise taxes.  Public water authorities have been\nsuccessful in protecting, preserving and enhancing the quality and quan-\ntity of water on Long Island. The formation of a public water authority\nshould be an option for the impacted localities to consider when evalu-\nating possible remedies to address the issues that have arisen with the\nprivate water utility. Municipalities within the NYAW service territory\nhave expressed interest, and also studied, in municipalizing their local\nservice territories. Their studies concur that municipalization is\nfeasible.\n\nThis legislation would authorize the establishment of the Nassau County\nWater Authority. Upon formation, the Authority would be authorized to\ntake over the assets and functions currently provided by the private\nutility. This legislation also exempts NYAW customers from the Special\nFranchise Tax for water utilities, alleviating the onerous property tax\nburden faced by NYAW customers.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2019/20: S6491-B/A9674-A (Similar, Passed Senate)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
